title: Why background tasks hold a weak reference
tags: Daemon [[Key invariants]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: The watcher and the executor must never keep their repository alive, or an unload would leak it with its store locked.

A loaded repository (`RepoState`) owns its store, and its store holds the
repository's exclusive lock. Its background tasks — the watcher's ingest thread,
the executor that applies buffered events — hold only a `Weak<RepoState>`, and
upgrade it for each piece of work.

If they held an `Arc`, the repository would keep itself alive through its own
threads: an unload would drop the daemon's reference and nothing else, the store
would never close, and its lock would stay held — a repository nobody can load
again, in this daemon or another, until the process exits.

This is a project invariant, checked by semgrep (`.semgrep/invariants.yml`,
"background tasks hold `Weak<RepoState>`").
